      <title>What are we failing to teach?</title>
      <link>https://www.dhakatribune.com/opinion/op-ed/425374/what-are-we-failing-to-teach</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.dhakatribune.com/opinion/op-ed/425374/what-are-we-failing-to-teach</guid>
      <pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:40:09 +0600</pubDate>
      <enclosure url="https://ecdn.dhakatribune.net/contents/cache/images/300x300x1/uploads/media/2026/11/22/28d83e28533300d7386e672edc86c9ae-6b01e51a8c837.jpeg?jadewits_media_id=78015" length="0" type="image/jpeg" />
      <media:content url="https://ecdn.dhakatribune.net/contents/cache/images/300x300x1/uploads/media/2026/11/22/28d83e28533300d7386e672edc86c9ae-6b01e51a8c837.jpeg?jadewits_media_id=78015" medium="image" />
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://ecdn.dhakatribune.net/contents/cache/images/300x300x1/uploads/media/2026/11/22/28d83e28533300d7386e672edc86c9ae-6b01e51a8c837.jpeg?jadewits_media_id=78015" style="max-width:100%;height:auto;margin-bottom:10px;" alt="What are we failing to teach?" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;“He who studies rides in cars and carriages.” 
This familiar Bengali proverb is something many of us grew up hearing. It reflects a traditional understanding of education: Study hard, achieve good results, secure a good job, and build a prosperous life. 
There is nothing wrong with that aspiration. &lt;/p&gt;</description>
